  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1232 PM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nOur wetter monsoonal pattern will gradually give way as the\nweekend progresses as drier mid-level air trickles in, with our \nmoisture plume becoming narrow and focusing itself near the \ncentral mountain chain in response. On Saturday, any convection \nwithin this high PWAT environment and unstable airmass could \npromote repeated convection over the same locations, increasing \nthe potential for locally heavy rainfall and runoff, particularly \nnear the Ruidoso burn scars and other vulnerable locations along \nthe higher terrain. By Sunday into Monday, the ridge is expected \nto strengthen and shift slightly westward, promoting slightly \nwarmer temperatures and suppressing overall storm coverage, mainly\nacross lower elevations. However, lingering moisture in the \nnorthern mountains and passing disturbances may allow for focused \nprecipitation chances along the eastern slopes of the Sangre de \nCristo Mountains and neighboring higher terrain heading into the \nnew work week. Forecast confidence moving into mid-to-late week \nremains low as long- term, ensemble, and AI guidance still vary on\nthe positioning of the mid-level ridge. As such, determining the \nultimate degree of drying, warming, and precipitation coverage \nacross the area is proving a bit difficult. The current NBM \nsolution is being used, suggesting more isolated storms and \ntemperatures above normal. Although, better refinements to the \nforecast can be expected once we get closer in time and model \nsolutions come into better agreement.",
